According to investigation done by basket team, this was caused by a possible attack from malicious users:

{quote}There were [85 unique users|http://kibana.internal/goto/9897ae82018b7d5ff432c7fe1fd86da5] sending the same request at least 10 times at the same minute, which was unusual.{quote}



[https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C02SWJK1ADU/p1649228459467299?thread_ts=1649153722.548049&cid=C02SWJK1ADU|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C02SWJK1ADU/p1649228459467299?thread_ts=1649153722.548049&cid=C02SWJK1ADU|smart-card]

{quote}I suppose we notice it if our monitoring or services are down{quote}

Not necessarily down, it may be a bug that stops calling reporting method or cron init, or metric group renaming, you name it.

{quote}But vehicles receive updates once in a minute when there’s no order, I think there will be a lot of false-positive alerts.{quote}

If it is once a minute, we could set a window to 90 sec or so. 
The earlier monitoring fires, the better.
Later on, that’s a good prerequisite for automatic deployments when we could start relying on monitoring to break the process if something goes off. Currently, it is still optimizing manual deployments trust.

{quote}Every vehicle update updates location (it is not just lat-lng, it is the whole vehicle cache state){quote}

I see, so it’s just a naming thing then, np.

{quote}Any problem in our IoT data processing is very bad{quote}

True. Yet ideally, monitoring allows pinpointing the root of an issue, and within this task, the goal was to monitor losing the update stream.

