Hey\!

Regarding the questions in the file:

# Return process

The idea is to conduct the return as follows:

·       Supplier delivers the goods

·       Bolt does not check the goods upon delivery but accepts them by signing the delivery note

·       Bolt identifies deficient goods within 24 hours as of the delivery of the goods

·       Bolt sends notification of the deficient goods to the supplier

·       Supplier issues a credit note for deficient goods

·       Bolt returns the deficient goods to the supplier

 

# Please confirm that there are no obstacles for using this process for returns under the local laws.

 

{color:#6554c0}_As a general answer – the proposed system is partially in compliance with EE, LV and LT regulations. The main issue being here – checking the goods. As a general principle, if goods are delivered, delivery note is signed and any visible damage to the goods should be stated on the landing bill. Any damage that cannot be seen, could be claimed later. But this part concerns only the shipping part._{color}

{color:#6554c0} {color}

{color:#6554c0}_We can agree on different terms with the supplier, but on the whole – they will keep the sipping terms and conditions in line with the applicable road transportation/carriage rules_{color}

 

# Our intention is to support the return process with the following documents:

a.     delivery note which is signed by Bolt and supplier upon delivery of the goods,

b.     email to the supplier regarding deficient goods and

c.     credit note issued by the supplier for deficient goods.

Please confirm that these documents and their form are sufficient in order to comply with local laws. If a signed document needs to be issued instead of an email under local laws, please let us know.

{color:#6554c0}_The delivery note is signed in three (3) copies, one copy for each party involved in the transaction. The terms and conditions of product quality and returns should be regulated in the sales agreement. As this will be triangular relationship, where we are submitting a claim against supplier and supplier against the carrier, there is the question regarding the burden of proof. Usually, any damage to the goods must be duly documented (as this can be question when the damage happened – if during the loading, the supplier is responsible, if during the shipping or unloading* - the carrier)._{color}

In addition, please let us know whether there is a mandatory requirement to have a signed document by the supplier as a proof of return of the goods under your local laws?

{color:#6554c0}_Please specify the set up – should we return the goods, what is the shipping term? If we are the ones responsible for the carriage, the agreement will be concluded between us and the carrier. If possible, EXW Bolt Market should be preferred condition for returning the goods._{color}

{color:#6554c0}_Regarding the signature/stamp:_{color}

{color:#6554c0}_EE - Unregulated_{color}

{color:#6554c0}_LV – compulsory_{color}

{color:#6554c0}_LT - compulsory_{color}

 

# Stock acceptance

Due to the quick nature of deliveries Bolt is unable to check every product upon delivery. Therefore, Bolt would like to accept all deliveries received by suppliers and notify the suppliers of the deficient goods within 24 hours after the receipt of the goods. Please confirm whether there are any legal obstacles to this approach under the local laws.

* If there is a mandatory obligation to check all the goods upon delivery, then please confirm whether the following clause in the supplier contract would release Bolt from liability for not checking the goods upon delivery?

_’Bolt has no obligation to check the goods delivered by supplier upon delivery. Acceptance of goods upon delivery shall in no way be considered as a waiver of claims regarding the quality or quantity of the goods. Bolt is entlitled to raise claims regarding deficient goods to the supplier within 24 hours after the delivery of the goods.’_

{color:#6554c0}_As this is triangular relationship, the supplier most likely demand that the shipping conditions are back-to-back in our agreements. Meaning – presume that hidden deficiencies could be claimed later, but visible damage to the goods must be documented on arrival._{color}

* In addition, will this clause in the delivery note release Bolt from liability for not checking the goods upon delivery?

_‘With this delivery note the supplier acknowledges that upon delivery Bolt has not inspected the quality, quantity, the packaging, the expiration date nor the health and safety of the delivered goods.’_

{color:#6554c0}_Most likely this clause will not hold up in the courts – shipping has long history and standards are set in place. This should be agreed in the sales agreement. As it increases the risks for the supplier, this will not -most likely- succeed._{color}

* Are there any other workarounds to release Bolt from liability for not checking the goods upon delivery?

{color:#6554c0}_Centralized service for logistics should be considered for mitigating the risks. Road carriage has specific rules and they should be followed – if no visible damage is seen, it is okay to accept the goods and later, if any discrepancy is discovered, a claim can be submitted._{color}

# *Storage of delivery notes*

* Is Bolt required to store originals of delivery notes or the copy (scanned delivery note) will suffice under the mandatory laws of your country?

{color:#6554c0}EE/LV/LT – No specific regulations{color}

* How long should Bolt store delivery notes under the mandatory laws of your country?

{color:#6554c0}EE – 1/3 years{color}

{color:#6554c0}LV – 3 years{color}

{color:#6554c0}LT – 2 years{color}

